# Can maths prove everything? Gödel's incompleteness theorems
subtitle: "How a 24-year-old turned sentences into numbers and proved that no rulebook for arithmetic can prove every truth about it."
tags: ["mathematics","logic","gödel incompleteness theorems","proof systems","mathematical foundations","self-reference"]
teaches: ["concepts/gödel-incompleteness","methods/gödel-numbering","concepts/self-referential-sentences","history/hilbert-program"]
thumb: "band \"This sentence is unprovable\" graph stamp \"TRUE.\""
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed","b":"gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace","take":"lines"}
prompt: "How can a sentence about numbers say 'I have no proof'? Why can't adding the missing truth as an axiom fix it?"
next: {"title":"Can a program tell whether any program will finish? Turing's halting problem","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/paradoxes/07-can-a-program-tell-whether-any-program.cast"}

## Gödel's incompleteness theorems
vars: {"p":110}
sources:
    - id: godel1931
      title: Über formal unentscheidbare Sätze der Principia Mathematica und verwandter Systeme I
      authors: Kurt Gödel
      year: 1931
      finding: 'Any consistent, effectively axiomatized system strong enough for arithmetic has true sentences it cannot prove, and cannot prove its own consistency (Monatshefte für Mathematik und Physik 38: 173–198).'
    - id: kirby_paris
      title: Accessible independence results for Peano arithmetic
      authors: Laurie Kirby and Jeff Paris
      year: 1982
      finding: 'Goodstein''s theorem is true but not provable in Peano arithmetic (Bull. London Math. Soc. 14: 285–293).'
    - id: nagel_newman
      title: Gödel's Proof
      authors: Ernest Nagel and James R. Newman
      year: 1958
      finding: The symbol codes used here (0 = 6, '=' = 5) and the number 243,000,000 for 0 = 0.

