    text d_g_l "G" #8a5fa8 x 575 y 445 font_size 34 hidden true
    shape d_g1 #8a5fa8 shape circle x 620 y 385 radius 11 style.fill #8a5fa8 hidden true
    text d_g1_l "G′" #8a5fa8 x 620 y 345 font_size 34 hidden true
    shape d_g2 #8a5fa8 shape circle x 700 y 430 radius 11 style.fill #8a5fa8 hidden true
    text d_g2_l "G″" #8a5fa8 x 700 y 470 font_size 34 hidden true
    shape d_con #8a5fa8 cites ["godel1931"] shape circle x 395 y 603 radius 11 style.fill #8a5fa8 hidden true
    text d_con_l "“I am consistent”" #8a5fa8 x 515 y 610 font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "Can maths prove everything?"

A: Here's a question that sounds easy: can one fixed set of rules (@draw q1@) prove every (@draw q2@) true statement (@draw q3@) about whole numbers?

B: Surely yes. If something's true about numbers, somebody can prove it, eventually.
    draw q_prov

    erase q1 q2 q3 q_prov

A: That was the great hope. Picture a machine: axioms go in, strict rules work on them, and out come theorems, each step checked.
    draw m_ax m_a1 m_ru m_a2 m_th

    flow m_a1 m_a2 duration 1.5

A: Its champion was David Hilbert, the leading mathematician of his day. He wanted the machine (@draw w_cons@) consistent, never proving a falsehood, and (@draw w_comp@) complete, proving every truth.
    draw hilbert

A: He was so sure that in September 1930, (@draw tag_k@) in Königsberg, he ended a famous speech with a motto: (@draw quote@) we must know, we will know.

A: The day before, at a round table in the same city, (@draw godel@) a twenty-four-year-old called Kurt Gödel had quietly announced that (@draw strike_comp@) it can't be done.

B: Quietly is right. Hardly anyone in the room noticed, apart from John von Neumann.
    highlight godel

    erase godel hilbert quote tag_k

A: Here's our route: turn sentences into numbers, build a sentence that talks about itself, then see why no new rule can rescue the machine.
    blocking false

    highlight m_ax duration 2.2

    highlight m_ru duration 2.2

    flow m_a1 m_a2 duration 2.5

    clear

    card title "Sentences into numbers"

B: Start with the numbers, then. How does a sentence become a number?
    draw t_h1 t_h2 t_rule

A: Give every symbol a code. Zero is six, and the equals sign is five.
    draw t_s1 t_c1 t_s2 t_c2

A: Take the tiny formula zero equals zero: three symbols, so the codes six, five, six.
    draw f_000 f_codes

A: Now glue them into one number: put the first code on the prime two, the second on three, the third on five.
    draw f_gn

    ask "Which tile finishes the code of 0 = 0?" on f_gn others ["6","3","2"] store tile voice a
        right: Five: the second symbol is the equals sign, and its code is five.
        wrong: Look at which symbol stands second.

A: Sixty-four, times two hundred and forty-three, times fifteen thousand six hundred and twenty-five: two hundred and forty-three million.
    draw f_val

B: That's a lot of number for 'zero equals zero'. Why bother?
    point at.ref f_val gesture underline

A: Because a number splits into primes only one way, you can always unpack it and get the formula back.
    draw f_one f_back f_unpack

B: So a whole proof, which is just a list of formulas, becomes one bigger number.
    draw p_proof p_arrow p_num

A: Yes, and 'this is a proof of that' turns into a statement about numbers, one the system itself can check.
    draw p_check

B: Hang on. So arithmetic can talk about its own proofs, without knowing that's what it's doing?
    highlight p_proof p_num

    clear

    card title "A sentence about itself"

A: Exactly, and Gödel used that to write a sentence, G, that says: G has no proof in this system.
    draw g_box

B: Isn't that just the liar paradox? 'This sentence is false', and round and round we go.
    draw liar

A: Close, but one word differs. The liar talks about truth; G talks about proof, and proof is something a machine can check.
    blocking false

    highlight liar duration 2.8

    highlight g_box duration 3.5

    erase liar

A: So there are just two cases. Suppose first that the system could prove G.
    draw e_prov n_prov

A: Then it would prove a sentence that says it has no proof: a falsehood. The machine would be broken.
    draw e_false n_false

    draw x_prov ruled_out

B: And the other case is that it can't prove G.
    draw e_unprov n_unprov

    ask "If the system never proves a falsehood, which case is G in? Tap it." choose ["n_prov","n_unprov"] answer n_unprov voice b
        right: Unprovable: the other branch broke the machine.

A: Unprovable. And no proof is exactly what G says about itself, so G is true.
    draw e_true n_true

    draw box_true

B: So it's true, we can see it's true, and the system itself can never prove it. No paradox at all.
    highlight g_box n_true

    clear

    card title "The gap that never closes"

A: So picture every statement about numbers: the true ones fill this circle, and the provable ones sit inside it.
    draw v_all v_all_l v_true v_true_l v_prov v_prov_l

A: G sits here, in the gap: true, but out of the machine's reach.
    draw d_g d_g_l

B: Easy fix, then. Add G as a new axiom, and now the machine proves it.
    animate p 150 duration 2.5 ghost ["v_prov"]

A: It does. But the new system can describe its own proofs too, so it has its own (@draw d_g1 d_g1_l@) sentence, G-prime, built the same way.

B: And if I add G-prime, there's a G-double-prime waiting.
    animate p 175 duration 2.5

    draw d_g2 d_g2_l

    erase v_prov_ghost

A: Every time. Any consistent rulebook for arithmetic, with axioms you can list, leaves a true sentence out.
    highlight d_g2

B: Hilbert had a fallback, though: could he at least prove his machine never contradicts itself?
    point at.ref v_prov gesture circle

A: That's Gödel's second theorem: a consistent system can't prove its own consistency. 'I (@draw d_con d_con_l@) am consistent' lands out here too.

A: None of this means nothing is certain, though. Almost everything (@draw v_ok@) mathematicians prove is proved as before; the theorem only says the rulebook has an edge.

A: And the gap holds more than riddles. In 1982, Paris and Kirby showed that Goodstein's theorem, about sequences that balloon and then fall back to zero, can't be proved from the usual axioms.
    shape d_good #8a5fa8 cites ["kirby_paris"] shape circle x 420 y 192 radius 11 style.fill #8a5fa8
    text d_good_l "Goodstein" #8a5fa8 cites ["kirby_paris"] x 535 y 195 font_size 26
    scratch good_seq top-right work ["Goodstein, from 4:","4, 26, 41, 60, 83 …","… then down to 0"] font_size 22

    erase good_seq

B: So the trouble isn't that we forgot an axiom. It's that the system is strong enough to talk about itself.
    point at.ref d_g gesture tap

A: Yes: once arithmetic can describe its own proofs, it can build a truth it can't reach, and every new axiom only moves the edge.
    highlight d_g d_g1 d_g2 d_con d_good

    quiz "A friend builds a new, consistent rulebook for arithmetic with a million axioms, all listed. What can you say?" voice b
        right: Some truths stay unprovable: Gödel's argument works for any listable, consistent set of axioms, however many.
        wrong: Think about what happened every time we added G as a new axiom.
        * It proves every true statement
        + Some truths stay unprovable
        * It must contradict itself

    clear

    card title "Can maths prove everything?"

A: So, back to where we started: can one set of rules prove every truth about numbers?
    draw q1 q2 q3 q_prov

A: No. And Gödel proved it using arithmetic itself.
    text end_no "No." #b5482e x 500 y 240 font_size 96

B: Not bad for a twenty-four-year-old.
    highlight end_no

