      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1r6tc6y'
    '|b||Hilbert had a fallback, though: could he at least prove his machine never contradicts itself?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5480
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:faw95i'
    '|a||That''s Gödel''s second theorem: a consistent system can''t prove its own consistency. ''I am consistent'' lands out here too.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7920
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:mz7e7g'
    '|a||None of this means nothing is certain, though. Almost everything mathematicians prove is proved as before; the theorem only says the rulebook has an edge.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8640
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1ct7ofp'
    '|a||And the gap holds more than riddles. In 1982, Paris and Kirby showed that Goodstein''s theorem, about sequences that balloon and then fall back to zero, can''t be proved from the usual axioms.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11520
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:diozzj'
    '|b||So the trouble isn''t that we forgot an axiom. It''s that the system is strong enough to talk about itself.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6080
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:es8sto'
    '|a||Yes: once arithmetic can describe its own proofs, it can build a truth it can''t reach, and every new axiom only moves the edge.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8360
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:akmf08'
    '|b||A friend builds a new, consistent rulebook for arithmetic with a million axioms, all listed. What can you say?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6520
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:18ilwco'
    '|b||Some truths stay unprovable: Gödel''s argument works for any listable, consistent set of axioms, however many.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7240
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace
    '|a||So, back to where we started: can one set of rules prove every truth about numbers?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5320
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||No. And Gödel proved it using arithmetic itself.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3520
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:19w29u6'
    '|b||Not bad for a twenty-four-year-old.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2080
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:osle6s'
